---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Welcome to Skateboarding"
  description="New to skateboarding? A friendly on-ramp for parents whose kids are just getting started."
>
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Parent Resources &rsaquo; Welcome to Skateboarding</p>
      <h1>You've got this. So does your kid.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        If your kid just asked for a skateboard, or you're curious whether
        skateboarding is right for them &mdash; you're in the right place.
      </p>

      <h2>What skateboarding gives kids</h2>
      <p>
        Skateboarding is one of the few kids' activities that's entirely
        self-paced and self-motivated. There's no coach yelling from the
        sideline, no bench to sit on &mdash; just a kid, a board, and a
        series of small challenges they choose to take on themselves. That
        structure builds real resilience: kids fall down learning to skate,
        a lot, and skateboarding teaches them that falling is just part of
        trying. Every small win (a first push, a first roll-in) is one they
        earned entirely on their own, which is where the confidence comes
        from.
      </p>

      <h2>Common worries, answered</h2>

      <h3>"Isn't skateboarding dangerous?"</h3>
      <p>
        Like any active sport, there's real risk &mdash; and real ways to
        manage it. Properly fitted safety gear (helmet, wrist guards, knee
        and elbow pads), a beginner-appropriate setup, and instruction from
        someone who teaches falling technique before tricks all make a
        meaningful difference. This is exactly what a certified instructor
        and a structured clinic are for.
      </p>

      <h3>"My kid has never stood on a board. Is that okay?"</h3>
      <p>
        That's exactly who our <a href="/programs/free-clinics">free clinics</a>
        are for. No experience or equipment needed &mdash; we start with
        balance and comfort before anything else.
      </p>

      <h3>"What age is a good age to start?"</h3>
      <p>
        Most kids can start learning fundamentals around age 5&ndash;6, once
        they're comfortable balancing and following simple instructions.
        There's no upper limit &mdash; plenty of kids (and adults) start
        much later and do great.
      </p>

      <h2>How to start</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Come try a <a href="/programs/free-clinics">free Learn to Skate clinic</a>
          &mdash; no gear or experience required.
        </li>
        <li>
          Read <a href="/parent-resources/first-trip-to-the-skatepark">your first trip to the skatepark</a>
          so you know what to expect.
        </li>
        <li>
          If your kid is hooked, look at our
          <a href="/programs/seasonal-sessions">seasonal sessions</a> for a
          real progression over time.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
